woi|n|A vessel propelled by oars, whether having masts and sails or not ; A large vessel for war and national purposes; -- common in the Middle Ages, and down to the 17th century. ; A name given by analogy to the Greek, Roman, and other ancient vessels propelled by oars. ; A light, open boat used on the Thames by customhouse officers, press gangs, and also for pleasure. ; One of the small boats carried by a man-of-war. ; The cookroom or kitchen and cooking apparatus of a vessel; -- sometimes on merchant vessels called the caboose. ; An oblong oven or muffle with a battery of retorts; a gallery furnace. ; An oblong tray of wood or brass, with upright sides, for holding type which has been set, or is to be made up, etc. ; A proof sheet taken from type while on a galley; a galley proof.	n:onf,39,3~onf,39,3	N:1res,110,41;1rel,84,21~1res,110,41;1rel,84,21
